======================================================================================================================
PART E -- PRICING THE LENGTH against what already constrains it
======================================================================================================================

  ONE HONESTY NOTE BEFORE ANY NUMBER.  The cluster data fix W'(U) only over the cluster's own range of U.
  Below that range W is a FREE function -- it has to fall to zero somewhere, but how is not measured.  So
  every number in this part is quoted at the REFERENCE value W'(U_cl), and each scales linearly with
  W'(U_sys)/W'(U_cl), which is unknown and is NOT assumed to be <= 1.  (At the lengths the gates require
  the measured p_local is near 1, so W' is roughly flat there; but that is an observation about the
  cluster range, not a bound below it.)  Where the margin is many orders of magnitude -- the Solar System
  and the wide binaries -- no continuation of W could bridge it and the conclusion is robust.  Where the
  margin is a factor of a few -- the groups -- it is NOT robust and the lane says so instead of scoring it.

      working point: kernel n4 (a realisable elliptic operator of order 4), l_rms = 1000 kpc

  E1 -- CASSINI AND THE WIDE BINARIES.  L56's window was EMPTY because Cassini capped the amplitude
       from BELOW: a weaker cluster slip needed a shallower weight and a shallow weight is nearly
       scale-free.  Here the suppression is a SCALE effect and needs no steepness at all, so that
       cap does not exist.  Checked, not asserted.
      Cassini, Saturn's orbit (9.58 AU)   : R = 2.480e-30   (1.1e-25 x Cassini)
      a wide binary at 10 kAU             : R = 2.821e-21   (1.2e-16 x Cassini)
      a globular cluster at 10 pc         : R = 2.476e-14   (1.1e-09 x Cassini)
      the Milky Way's OWN slip at 8.2 kpc : R = 1.364e-05   (5.9e-01 x Cassini)
  [PASS] E1 [test] the mechanism passes CASSINI and the wide-binary regime   (YES by 9.3e+24 at Cassini and 8.2e+15 at 10 kAU, on both footings, with NO steepness assumed anywhere -- the suppression is (R_p/r_cl)^3 and a Solar-System probe radius is 4.6e-11 of a cluster radius.  L56 E3's pincer, in which Cassini capped the amplitude from below, is LIFTED.  This is the second real positive of the lane)

  E2 -- DISC OUTSKIRTS AND ISOLATED FIELD GALAXIES, on the real SPARC profiles.
      155 SPARC galaxies, each given its OWN measured baryonic mass and its own size (r_d = R_last/4),
      probed out to 3 R_last (capped at 300 kpc):
      reference response R = 8.432e-07 - 3.619e-01 (median 1.233e-03)
  [FAIL] E2 [test] disc outskirts and isolated field galaxies keep the deposited theory's no-slip result at the working point (kernel n4, l_rms = 1 Mpc)   (the reference response reaches 3.62e-01, i.e. 3619 x the theory's own 1e-4 and 1.81 x the 20% empirical bound, while the median galaxy sits at 1.2e-03 and 153/155 clear the 20% bound.  The largest values belong to the physically largest discs, probed to 300 kpc, exactly as the (R_p/r_cl)^3 law says they must: at THIS working point (l_rms = 1 Mpc) the empirical gate holds out to about 150 kpc and not to 300 kpc, and reaching 300 kpc needs l_rms >= 2 Mpc (C6).  The theory-arm FAIL is the same one C5a records and is not independent of it)

  E3 -- X-RAY GROUPS, which is where the mechanism makes its sharpest prediction.
      20 X-ray groups (Lovisari, Reiprich & Schellenberger 2015 Tables 1-2), each modelled
      as an isothermal sphere rho ~ r^-2 truncated at 2 R500 and normalised to M_gas500 x 1.30 --
      a STATED model, and the one that maximises the group's own trigger gradient
      bounded group response R = 2.147e-01 - 4.504e-01 (median 3.078e-01)
  [FAIL] E3 [test] X-ray groups keep the deposited theory's no-slip result at the working point   (NO -- the bound reaches 4.50e-01, 5e+03 x the theory's own 1e-4 and 2.25 x the 20% empirical bound (median 1.54 x).  Groups are EXTENDED against the smoothing, so the mechanism does not hide them -- that is not a bug, it is the mechanism working as designed, and it is the sharpest thing it predicts: a lensing-versus-hydrostatic discrepancy in GROUPS of order 31%.  Whether that is excluded is NOT decided here: this repository's own group-scale estimator disagreement (KT2017 vs g06, +0.42 dex on 19 shared hosts) is larger than the effect, so the channel is estimator-limited and the lane refuses to score it in either direction)

  E4 -- THE EXTERNAL FIELD.  A nonlocal trigger IS an external-field effect on the slip: a galaxy's
       weight is set by everything within l_rms of it.  That is a prediction, and it is testable.
      smoothed trigger at l_rms = 1000 kpc:  cluster at 1 Mpc 4.560e-25 kg/m^3;  an isolated 1e11 Msun galaxy 4.372e-27;  cosmic mean 4.181e-28
      separation cluster : field galaxy = 104.3 x, and the field galaxy sits 10.46 x the cosmic mean
      => the weight at a field galaxy is smaller than at a cluster by 104^p_local ~ 135 x, so the predicted galaxy-galaxy-lensing slip is strongly environment-dependent: at the working point, 2.25% for a galaxy at cluster
         radii (0.024% at the floor) against essentially zero in the field.
  [FAIL] E4 [test] the environmental dependence the mechanism predicts is already excluded by measurement   (NOT DECIDED, and this is the honest state of it.  The mechanism predicts a lensing-versus-dynamical discrepancy of 0.024% - 2.3% for galaxies sitting at cluster radii and essentially none in the field.  Galaxy-galaxy lensing of cluster members is not measured anywhere near 0.1%, so the prediction is neither confirmed nor refuted.  It is, however, the measurement that would decide this door, and it is named here as such)

  E5 -- THE COSMOLOGICAL BACKGROUND.  A normalised kernel leaves a homogeneous universe exactly
       where it is (A7), so the background trigger is the cosmic mean and W there is off.  But a
       linear perturbation of wavelength LONGER than l_rms is not smoothed at all, so it is not
       suppressed.  Bounded here with the cluster's own W'.
      a delta = 1 perturbation on 10 Mpc: g_fw = 1.31e-12 m/s^2, bounded slip response R = 2.570e-03
  [PASS] E5 [test] the REFERENCE cosmological slip on 10 Mpc scales is under 1%   (bounded at 2.57e-03, i.e. a gravitational slip of order 0.3% on 10 Mpc scales.  The bound uses the CLUSTER's W' and is therefore a large over-estimate -- the true value depends on W below the cluster range, which is unconstrained -- so this is reported as an open channel with a named calculation (a Limber cosmic-shear forecast with the pinned W), NOT as a kill and NOT as a pass)

======================================================================================================================
PART F -- THE RUN: the best achievable shear-shape improvement, as a curve
======================================================================================================================

  Four constraints act on the amplitude f of the added lensing phantom.  Unlike L56, only ONE of them is
  a real cap here:
    (1) the lensing-to-dynamics ratio (measured 1.148 +/- 0.146 against the theory's 1.000) caps f ABOVE;
    (2) the embedded-galaxy consistency R_emb = f x (M_P/M_fw) x S caps f ABOVE;
    (3) CASSINI -- which emptied L56's window by capping f from BELOW -- does not cap f at all (E1),
        because the suppression is geometric and needs no steep weight;
    (4) the isolated-galaxy gate is implied by (2) and does not bind separately.

